{"text": " It's like your old boss posting about you on LinkedIn and tagging your current employer. And Besant didn't take it well. Druckenmiller and Besant aren't just two guys who know each other. They are mentor and protege. Druckenmiller is a billionaire hedge fund manager and widely considered one of the greatest investors of all time. Scott Besant got his start in finance working for Stanley Druckenmiller. He hired Scott Besant at Soros in 1991. So this isn't a critic the Treasury Secretary can wave off. This is the man he learned the job from in the Wall Street Journal. explaining that he's doing it wrong. He answered a week later on television from a G20 meeting in Asheville. He said, Stans a great investor, he changes his mind a lot, and he doesn't like losing money. I think he lost money the day he sent in the editorial. Besant called him by his nickname and then told him the financial equivalent of cry about it. So we have one man saying that the market price is information and you're supposed to listen to it. The other says the price is an opinion and policy doesn't take orders from opinions. If Besant isn't going to listen to the market, then what exactly is the plan?", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 4.34, "text": " It's like your old boss posting about you on LinkedIn and tagging your current employer.", "tokens": [50363, 632, 338, 588, 534, 1468, 6478, 10754, 546, 345, 319, 27133, 290, 49620, 534, 1459, 9749, 13, 50587], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16888020662653366, "compression_ratio": 1.6873156342182891, "no_speech_prob": 0.006598226260393858, "words": [{"word": " It's", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.22, "probability": 0.8510628640651703}, {"word": " like", "start": 0.22, "end": 0.38, "probability": 0.9939919114112854}, {"word": " your", "start": 0.38, "end": 0.52, "probability": 0.9619554281234741}, {"word": " old", "start": 0.52, "end": 0.74, "probability": 0.9892469048500061}, {"word": " boss", "start": 0.74, "end": 1.